const CoursePractice={"updated":"27 October 2026","exercises":[{"id":"hw1-benchmark-fill-advance","title":"Shuttle fares: a full vehicle or the best revenue?","lectureIds":["foundations"],"topicIds":["airline-demand","segmentation"],"kind":"homework","assignment":"Homework 1 · parts (a)-(c)","sources":["Assignment1.pdf · pages 2-4, parts (a)-(c); worked solution derived from the stated model"],"prompt":"One airport-shuttle departure has 40 seats. Each passenger occupies one seat and receives the same service. Regular tickets are bought less than seven days ahead. Advance tickets are bought at least seven days ahead, including exactly seven days, and are nonrefundable and unchangeable. Demand is deterministic. Ignore operating costs and maximize ticket revenue. Customers do not switch between the regular and advance streams. Choose fares so that all demand is served and total demand does not exceed 40; there is no rationing or overbooking decision. Treat passenger quantities as continuous. Keep exact values until the final answer, and round final money amounts to two decimals when needed. Load factor is total passengers divided by 40.\n$$D_W(p)=\\begin{cases}54-1.5p&0\\le p\\le30,\\\\24-0.5p&30<p\\le48,\\\\0&p>48.\\end{cases}$$\n$$D_A(p)=48-2p,\\qquad0\\le p\\le24.$$\nAll prices are in dollars. Current fares are pW = $36 and pA = $18. Keep the regular fare fixed at $36 in every part of this exercise.","tables":[],"tasks":["(a) At the current fares, find regular demand, advance demand, total seats sold, load factor and total revenue.","(b) Change only the advance fare so that total demand exactly fills the 40-seat shuttle. Find that fare, demand for each product and revenue. Compare revenue with (a).","(c) Keep the regular fare at $36, but choose the advance fare to maximize revenue subject to its price domain and capacity. Find the fare, demand, revenue and load factor. Compare with (b), and explain why load factor alone can be a misleading objective."],"hints":[{"title":"Choose the correct branch","body":"First evaluate regular demand at its fixed fare. It remains unchanged when only the advance fare changes."},{"title":"Distinguish filling from optimizing","body":"For (b), set total demand equal to 40. For (c), maximize fixed regular revenue plus advance fare times advance demand, using the capacity inequality rather than an equality."},{"title":"Check the stationary point and its domain","body":"For (c), substitute the fixed regular demand into capacity to obtain a lower bound on pA. Differentiate the advance-revenue quadratic and check whether its stationary point satisfies that bound and 0 ≤ pA ≤ 24."}],"solution":{"label":"Derived worked solution","body":"The same demand functions apply in all three policies. Filling all seats is a constraint in (b), but revenue is the objective in (c).","sections":[{"title":"(a) Current fares","body":"The regular fare is above $30, so qW = 24 − 0.5(36) = 6. Advance demand is qA = 48 − 2(18) = 12. Total demand is 18 and load factor is 18/40 = 45%.\n$$R=36(6)+18(12)=432.$$"},{"title":"(b) Fill capacity","body":"Regular demand stays at 6. Thus advance demand must be 40 − 6 = 34. Solving 48 − 2pA = 34 gives pA = $7.\n$$R=36(6)+7(34)=454.$$\nThe shuttle is full and revenue exceeds (a) by $22."},{"title":"(c) Optimize advance revenue","body":"Capacity requires 6 + 48 − 2pA ≤ 40, so 7 ≤ pA ≤ 24. The objective is\n$$R(p_A)=216+48p_A-2p_A^2.$$\nIts derivative is 48 − 4pA, and its second derivative is −4. The feasible maximizer is pA = $12. Then qA = 24, total demand is 30, load factor is 75%, and revenue is 216 + 12(24) = $504.\nThis is $50 more than filling the shuttle, despite selling ten fewer tickets. The higher advance fare more than compensates for lower advance demand. A load-factor objective can prefer a policy with lower ticket revenue."}],"tables":[{"caption":"Compare the three policies","headers":["Part","Regular fare ($)","Advance fare ($)","Regular / advance passengers","Load factor","Revenue ($)"],"rows":[["a","36","18","6 / 12","45%","432.00"],["b","36","7","6 / 34","100%","454.00"],["c","36","12","6 / 24","75%","504.00"]]}]},"compare":["Did you use the upper-price regular-demand branch at pW = $36?","Did you keep regular demand fixed while changing pA?","For (c), did you check both the advance price domain and the capacity inequality rather than forcing a full shuttle?","Are your revenues sums of fare × demand, and are your load factors divided by 40?"]},{"id":"hw1-joint-two-fares","title":"Shuttle fares: optimize both products","lectureIds":["foundations"],"topicIds":["airline-demand","segmentation"],"kind":"homework","assignment":"Homework 1 · part (d)","sources":["Assignment1.pdf · pages 2-4, part (d); worked solution derived from the stated model"],"prompt":"One airport-shuttle departure has 40 seats. Each passenger occupies one seat and receives the same service. Regular tickets are bought less than seven days ahead. Advance tickets are bought at least seven days ahead, including exactly seven days, and are nonrefundable and unchangeable. Demand is deterministic. Ignore operating costs and maximize ticket revenue. Customers do not switch between the regular and advance streams. Choose fares so that all demand is served and total demand does not exceed 40; there is no rationing or overbooking decision. Treat passenger quantities as continuous. Keep exact values until the final answer, and round final money amounts to two decimals when needed. Load factor is total passengers divided by 40.\n$$D_W(p)=\\begin{cases}54-1.5p&0\\le p\\le30,\\\\24-0.5p&30<p\\le48,\\\\0&p>48.\\end{cases}$$\n$$D_A(p)=48-2p,\\qquad0\\le p\\le24.$$\nChoose both fares, with 0 ≤ pW ≤ 48 and 0 ≤ pA ≤ 24. Charge one common regular fare to everyone in the regular stream. Regular fares above $48 add no demand outcome beyond pW = $48.\n$$\\max_{p_W,p_A}\\;p_WD_W(p_W)+p_AD_A(p_A),\\qquad D_W(p_W)+D_A(p_A)\\le40.$$","tables":[],"tasks":["Find the optimal two fares, demand for each product, total revenue and load factor.","Determine whether capacity binds, and justify that claim.","Verify that the candidate regular fare belongs to the demand branch used to derive it. Compare it with the best outcome on the other branch, including the branch boundary."],"hints":[{"title":"Separate the branches","body":"Solve once for 0 ≤ pW ≤ 30 and once for 30 < pW ≤ 48. The two branch expressions agree at the boundary, but their inverse-demand slopes differ."},{"title":"Use quantities on the low-price branch","body":"There, pW = 36 − (2/3)qW and pA = 24 − qA/2. Translate the price domains into quantity bounds before imposing qW + qA ≤ 40."},{"title":"Compare marginal revenue and endpoints","body":"First check the unconstrained quantity maxima against capacity. If capacity binds and both quantities are interior, equate marginal revenues. On the other branch, inspect its permitted endpoint and the best advance choice."}],"solution":{"label":"Derived worked solution","body":"The optimal regular fare is on the lower-price branch. A separate branch comparison is required because the aggregate regular demand is piecewise.","sections":[{"title":"Lower-price branch","body":"For 0 ≤ pW ≤ 30, write\n$$p_W=36-\\tfrac23q_W,\\qquad p_A=24-\\tfrac12q_A.$$\nThe quantity bounds are 9 ≤ qW ≤ 54 and 0 ≤ qA ≤ 48. Revenue is\n$$R=36q_W-\\tfrac23q_W^2+24q_A-\\tfrac12q_A^2.$$\nThe unconstrained maxima qW = 27 and qA = 24 total 51, exceeding capacity. Strict concavity and positive marginal revenues at the constrained optimum imply binding capacity. Set qA = 40 − qW and equate marginal revenues:\n$$36-\\tfrac43q_W=24-q_A,\\qquad q_W+q_A=40.$$\nThus qW = 156/7 and qA = 124/7. The fares are pW = 148/7 and pA = 106/7, or approximately $21.14 and $15.14. The regular fare is below $30, as required. Total revenue is 5176/7 ≈ $739.43, with 100% load factor."},{"title":"Higher-price branch and global comparison","body":"For 30 < pW ≤ 48, regular demand satisfies 0 ≤ qW < 9 and its inverse demand is pW = 48 − 2qW. Its revenue 48qW − 2qW² is increasing throughout this quantity interval. Advance revenue is maximized at qA = 24; even as qW approaches 9, total demand approaches only 33, so capacity does not restrict this branch.\nIncluding the boundary pW = $30 gives the limiting best policy pA = $12, with revenue 30(9) + 12(24) = $558. On the strictly open branch pW > 30, $558 is a supremum rather than an attained maximum. Both are below $739.43, proving the lower-branch result is globally best."}],"tables":[{"caption":"Optimal two-fare policy","headers":["Product","Fare ($), exact / approximate","Passengers, exact / approximate"],"rows":[["Regular","148/7 / 21.142857","156/7 / 22.285714"],["Advance","106/7 / 15.142857","124/7 / 17.714286"]]}]},"compare":["Did you solve and compare both regular-demand branches?","Does your candidate fare lie in the branch used to calculate it?","Do the exact quantities sum to 40 before rounding?","Did you distinguish the open higher-price branch from its pW = $30 boundary?","Did you justify optimality using concavity, marginal revenue and the branch comparison?"]},{"id":"hw1-student-fare","title":"Shuttle fares: introduce a student product","lectureIds":["foundations"],"topicIds":["segmentation","app-fare"],"kind":"homework","assignment":"Homework 1 · part (e)","sources":["Assignment1.pdf · pages 2, 4-5, part (e); worked solution derived from the stated model"],"prompt":"One airport-shuttle departure has 40 seats. Each passenger occupies one seat and receives the same service. Regular tickets are bought less than seven days ahead. Advance tickets are bought at least seven days ahead, including exactly seven days, and are nonrefundable and unchangeable. Demand is deterministic. Ignore operating costs and maximize ticket revenue. Customers do not switch between the regular and advance streams. Choose fares so that all demand is served and total demand does not exceed 40; there is no rationing or overbooking decision. Treat passenger quantities as continuous. Keep exact values until the final answer, and round final money amounts to two decimals when needed. Load factor is total passengers divided by 40.\nThe original regular stream consists of nonstudents R and students S; students are already included in regular demand, not an additional population. Students show a valid student ID and buy the student product at the fares considered. Nonstudents cannot use the student fare. No customers switch between the regular/student stream and advance purchases. Nonstudent demand is zero above $48; student demand is zero above $30.\n$$D_R(p_R)=24-\\tfrac12p_R,\\quad0\\le p_R\\le48;\\qquad D_S(p_S)=30-p_S,\\quad0\\le p_S\\le30.$$\n$$D_A(p_A)=48-2p_A,\\qquad0\\le p_A\\le24.$$\nThe company fixes pR = $30 and pS = $18, then chooses pA to maximize revenue. Fare decisions concern the same departure; their descriptive order is not a chronological booking order.\nFor the unsegmented comparison, charge one common fare pW ∈ [0,48] to both R and S, using qW = DR(pW) + DS(pW), and optimize pW jointly with pA. This comparison is fully specified by the same group demands above.","tables":[],"tasks":["Find nonstudent regular demand and student demand at the fixed fares.","Find the revenue-maximizing advance fare, advance demand and total revenue subject to capacity.","Explain how the fixed fares affect seats available to advance customers, without assuming an arrival order.","Show how equal R and S fares reproduce the two branches of the original aggregate regular demand.","Compare revenue with the best unsegmented common-regular-fare policy, solving that benchmark from the supplied group demands if needed."],"hints":[{"title":"Use the correct customer population","body":"Compute R and S demand separately. Do not add student demand to the old aggregate DW, because the students were already included in it."},{"title":"Compute residual capacity","body":"Subtract the demand generated by the fixed R and S fares from 40. Compare that remainder with the unconstrained maximum of advance revenue."},{"title":"Interpret the common-fare benchmark","body":"At a common regular fare at most $30, add both linear group demands. Above $30, student demand is zero, so only nonstudent demand remains. Optimize those two branches with advance demand for the comparison."}],"solution":{"label":"Derived worked solution","body":"The fixed segmented fares leave a capacity bound on advance demand. The price decisions do not imply which group books first.","sections":[{"title":"Segment demand and advance optimization","body":"At pR = $30, qR = 24 − 30/2 = 9. At pS = $18, qS = 30 − 18 = 12. Together they demand 21 seats, leaving at most 19 for advance passengers.\nAdvance revenue in quantity form is 24qA − qA²/2. Its unconstrained maximum is at qA = 24, exceeding the remaining capacity. Revenue is increasing up to that quantity, so choose qA = 19. The inverse demand gives pA = 24 − 19/2 = $14.50.\n$$R=30(9)+18(12)+14.5(19)=761.50.$$\nAll 40 seats are used. The residual 19-seat calculation follows from simultaneous demands at the fixed fares, not a sequential reservation assumption."},{"title":"Aggregate demand and the unsegmented comparison","body":"If both regular groups pay p ≤ $30, DR(p) + DS(p) = (24 − p/2) + (30 − p) = 54 − 1.5p. For 30 < p ≤ 48, students demand zero and only 24 − p/2 remains.\nFor the common-fare benchmark, the lower-branch inverse demands are pW = 36 − (2/3)qW and pA = 24 − qA/2. Equating marginal revenues with qW + qA = 40 gives qW = 156/7 and qA = 124/7, with fares 148/7 and 106/7. The higher branch cannot exceed the boundary revenue $558. Thus the unsegmented optimum is 5176/7 ≈ $739.43.\nFixed segmented fares improve revenue by 761.50 − 5176/7 = 309/14 ≈ $22.07. The comparison holds the customer populations and physical service fixed."}],"tables":[{"caption":"Fixed segmented policy","headers":["Product","Fare ($)","Demand"],"rows":[["Nonstudent regular","30","9"],["Student","18","12"],["Advance","14.50","19"]]}]},"compare":["Did you avoid double-counting students inside both DW and DS?","Did your advance optimum respect residual capacity and the advance price domain?","Do all three demand quantities sum to no more than 40?","Did you compare with an optimized common regular fare, rather than the original pW = $36 benchmark?","Did you explain the fare-ordering point without inventing a chronological sales process?"]},{"id":"hw1-joint-three-fares","title":"Shuttle fares: optimize all three products","lectureIds":["foundations"],"topicIds":["segmentation","app-fare"],"kind":"homework","assignment":"Homework 1 · part (f)","sources":["Assignment1.pdf · pages 2, 4-5, part (f); worked solution derived from the stated model"],"prompt":"One airport-shuttle departure has 40 seats. Each passenger occupies one seat and receives the same service. Regular tickets are bought less than seven days ahead. Advance tickets are bought at least seven days ahead, including exactly seven days, and are nonrefundable and unchangeable. Demand is deterministic. Ignore operating costs and maximize ticket revenue. Customers do not switch between the regular and advance streams. Choose fares so that all demand is served and total demand does not exceed 40; there is no rationing or overbooking decision. Treat passenger quantities as continuous. Keep exact values until the final answer, and round final money amounts to two decimals when needed. Load factor is total passengers divided by 40.\nThe original regular stream consists of nonstudents R and students S; students are already included in regular demand, not an additional population. Students show a valid student ID and buy the student product at the fares considered. Nonstudents cannot use the student fare. No customers switch between the regular/student stream and advance purchases. Nonstudent demand is zero above $48; student demand is zero above $30.\n$$D_R(p_R)=24-\\tfrac12p_R,\\quad0\\le p_R\\le48;\\qquad D_S(p_S)=30-p_S,\\quad0\\le p_S\\le30.$$\n$$D_A(p_A)=48-2p_A,\\qquad0\\le p_A\\le24.$$\nChoose all three fares. Equivalently, use the supplied inverse demands and quantity bounds:\n$$p_R=48-2q_R,\\qquad p_S=30-q_S,\\qquad p_A=24-\\tfrac12q_A.$$\n$$0\\le q_R\\le24,\\qquad0\\le q_S\\le30,\\qquad0\\le q_A\\le48,\\qquad q_R+q_S+q_A\\le40.$$\nFor comparison, the unsegmented policy sets a single common regular fare pW ∈ [0,48] for R and S and optimizes it jointly with pA. The fixed segmented policy sets pR = $30 and pS = $18 while optimizing only pA. Both comparison policies use the same demands and 40-seat capacity; no previous exercise is needed to specify them.","tables":[],"tasks":["Formulate total ticket revenue in quantities, then find all three optimal quantities and fares.","Calculate total revenue and load factor, and determine whether capacity binds.","Justify global optimality and explain the economic reason for the seat allocation.","Compare revenue with both the best unsegmented common-regular-fare policy and the fixed segmented policy defined above."],"hints":[{"title":"Check the unconstrained maxima","body":"Write each price-times-quantity expression and maximize it independently. Compare the sum of these quantities with the 40 seats."},{"title":"Use marginal revenue for a binding capacity","body":"At an interior optimum with shared binding capacity, marginal revenues should be equal. Derive them from each quadratic revenue expression."},{"title":"Use a common multiplier","body":"Solve 48 − 4qR = 30 − 2qS = 24 − qA = λ together with the binding capacity equation, then check every quantity bound. Strict concavity gives a global optimality argument."}],"solution":{"label":"Derived worked solution","body":"The three revenue quadratics are strictly concave, and the shared capacity is binding at the optimum.","sections":[{"title":"Quantity formulation and capacity","body":"$$\\max R=48q_R-2q_R^2+30q_S-q_S^2+24q_A-\\tfrac12q_A^2.$$\nUse the quantity bounds and shared capacity from the question. Without capacity, the separate maxima are qR = 12, qS = 15 and qA = 24, totaling 51. A constrained optimum must allocate the scarce seats across the groups."},{"title":"Equal marginal revenues","body":"For interior quantities, the first-order conditions are\n$$48-4q_R=30-2q_S=24-q_A=\\lambda.$$\nHence qR = 12 − λ/4, qS = 15 − λ/2 and qA = 24 − λ. Their sum is 51 − 7λ/4. Setting it to 40 gives λ = 44/7 > 0 and\n$$(q_R,q_S,q_A)=\\left(\\tfrac{73}{7},\\tfrac{83}{7},\\tfrac{124}{7}\\right).$$\nEvery quantity is strictly within its bounds. Substitution into inverse demand gives\n$$(p_R,p_S,p_A)=\\left(\\tfrac{190}{7},\\tfrac{127}{7},\\tfrac{106}{7}\\right).$$\nTotal demand is exactly 40, load factor is 100%, and revenue is 5365/7 ≈ $766.43. The objective Hessian has diagonal entries −4, −2 and −1, so the concave first-order solution is the unique global quantity optimum on the convex feasible set. Equal marginal revenues mean that reallocating a small seat quantity between any two groups cannot increase total revenue."},{"title":"Comparison policies","body":"For the unsegmented policy, adding the R and S demands gives DW = 54 − 1.5pW up to $30 and DW = 24 − 0.5pW above $30. On the lower branch, equating marginal revenues under capacity gives qW = 156/7 and qA = 124/7; the corresponding revenue is 5176/7 ≈ $739.43. The other branch has boundary revenue at most $558.\nFor the fixed segmented policy, pR = $30 and pS = $18 produce demands 9 and 12, leaving 19 seats for advance passengers. The best feasible advance fare is $14.50, giving revenue $761.50.\nFull three-fare optimization therefore improves revenue by exactly $27 over unsegmented optimization and by 69/14 ≈ $4.93 over the fixed segmented policy."}],"tables":[{"caption":"Optimal three-fare policy","headers":["Group","Quantity, exact / approximate","Fare ($), exact / approximate"],"rows":[["Nonstudent regular","73/7 / 10.428571","190/7 / 27.142857"],["Student","83/7 / 11.857143","127/7 / 18.142857"],["Advance","124/7 / 17.714286","106/7 / 15.142857"]]}]},"compare":["Did you retain all price/quantity bounds and the same customer populations?","Do your exact quantities sum to 40, and do all inverse-demand substitutions agree?","Are all three marginal revenues equal and the shared-capacity multiplier positive?","Did you use concavity to justify global optimality rather than only report a stationary point?","Did you compare like-for-like revenues with the two specified policies before rounding?"]},{"id":"hw2-graphical-maximum","title":"Graphical LP: a bounded maximum","lectureIds":["linear-programming"],"topicIds":["graphical","pottery"],"kind":"homework","assignment":"Homework 2 · problem 1","sources":["Assignment 2.pdf · page 2, problem 1","New Answer of assignment 2.pdf · page 1, official key"],"prompt":"Solve the following continuous linear program graphically. Both variables are nonnegative.\n$$\\max Z=5x_1+8x_2.$$\n$$3x_1+5x_2\\le50,\\qquad2x_1+4x_2\\le40,\\qquad x_1\\le8,\\qquad x_2\\le10,\\qquad x_1,x_2\\ge0.$$","tables":[],"tasks":["Draw every boundary and identify the feasible region.","Find its feasible corner points and evaluate the objective there.","Report the optimal point and objective value, and identify the constraints binding at the optimum."],"hints":[{"title":"Plot intercepts and bounds","body":"Draw the two resource lines, the vertical x1 upper bound and horizontal x2 upper bound. Keep only the part of the first quadrant satisfying every inequality."},{"title":"Remove infeasible intersections","body":"Some pairwise intersections or intercepts can violate another bound. Check each candidate against all constraints before using it as a corner."},{"title":"Compare every feasible vertex","body":"Evaluate 5x1 + 8x2 at each vertex. The best point must be compared with both axis endpoints and the origin, not merely found as an intersection."}],"solution":{"label":"Derived worked solution","body":"The result agrees with the official graphical key. The vertex calculations below make the comparison explicit.","sections":[{"title":"Feasible polygon and objective values","body":"For x1 ≥ 0, the first resource gives x2 ≤ 10 − 0.6x1, which is at least as restrictive as x2 ≤ 10 − 0.5x1 from the second resource. Intersect this lower boundary with 0 ≤ x1 ≤ 8 and x2 ≥ 0.\nThe vertices are (0,0), (8,0), (8,26/5) and (0,10). Their objective values are 0, 40, 408/5 and 80."},{"title":"Optimum and substitution check","body":"The unique maximum is (x1,x2) = (8,5.2), with Z = 81.6. The first resource binds because 3(8) + 5(5.2) = 50, and x1 = 8 binds. The second resource uses 2(8) + 4(5.2) = 36.8, leaving slack 3.2. The x2 upper bound leaves slack 4.8. Both variables are positive."}],"tables":[{"caption":"Vertex comparison","headers":["Vertex","Objective Z"],"rows":[["(0,0)","0"],["(8,0)","40"],["(8,5.2)","81.6"],["(0,10)","80"]]}],"visual":{"topicId":"a2-graphical","figure":0}},"compare":["Did your shaded region satisfy every bound, including nonnegativity?","Did you reject intersections outside x1 ≤ 8 or above a resource boundary?","Did you include all four feasible vertices in the objective comparison?","Does substituting your optimum show exactly which constraints bind?"]},{"id":"hw2-unbounded-and-minimum","title":"Graphical LP: reverse the objective sense","lectureIds":["linear-programming"],"topicIds":["irregular","graphical"],"kind":"homework","assignment":"Homework 2 · problem 2","sources":["Assignment 2.pdf · page 2, problem 2","New Answer of assignment 2.pdf · pages 1-2, official key"],"prompt":"Use the same feasible region for both parts. Variables are continuous and nonnegative.\n$$Z=3x_1+2x_2.$$\n$$2x_1+4x_2\\ge22,\\qquad-x_1+4x_2\\ge10,\\qquad4x_1-2x_2\\le14,\\qquad x_1-3x_2\\le1,\\qquad x_1,x_2\\ge0.$$","tables":[],"tasks":["(a) Maximize Z graphically. State whether a finite maximum exists, and justify your conclusion.","(b) Change only the objective sense to minimization. Find the optimal point and value.","Show the relevant boundary intersections and distinguish an unbounded feasible region from an unbounded objective."],"hints":[{"title":"Rewrite as lower bounds on x2","body":"Rearrange each inequality into the form x2 ≥ a + b x1 and include x2 ≥ 0. The feasible region lies above their upper envelope."},{"title":"Check an improving ray","body":"For maximization, do not stop after evaluating corner points. Test whether increasing one variable from a feasible point remains feasible and increases Z indefinitely."},{"title":"Use corners for the minimum","body":"Find where the active lower-boundary lines change. Compare the objective at those vertices, then check the direction of growth along the unbounded rays."}],"solution":{"label":"Derived worked solution","body":"The official key reports an unbounded maximum and the finite minimum (0,5.5), Z = 11. The ray below supplies the required unboundedness argument.","sections":[{"title":"Boundary and maximization","body":"The four row restrictions become x2 ≥ 5.5 − 0.5x1, x2 ≥ 2.5 + 0.25x1, x2 ≥ 2x1 − 7 and x2 ≥ (x1 − 1)/3. The active lower-boundary vertices are (0,11/2), (4,7/2) and (38/7,27/7).\nFor any t ≥ 0, (x1,x2) = (0,11/2 + t) satisfies every inequality. Its objective is Z = 11 + 2t, which grows without bound. Therefore there is no finite maximizing optimum."},{"title":"Minimization","body":"The three boundary vertex values are 11, 19 and 24. The unbounded rays cannot improve on the first value for this positive-cost objective. Thus the minimum is (0,11/2), with Z = 11.\nA direct bound also proves it: the first row gives 0.5x1 + x2 ≥ 5.5, so 3x1 + 2x2 = 2(0.5x1 + x2) + 2x1 ≥ 11 for x1 ≥ 0. The reported point attains that bound."}],"tables":[{"caption":"Lower-boundary vertices","headers":["Vertex","Objective Z"],"rows":[["(0,11/2)","11"],["(4,7/2)","19"],["(38/7,27/7)","24"]]}],"visual":{"topicId":"a2-graphical","figure":1}},"compare":["Did you keep the correct inequality sides when drawing the feasible region?","For maximization, did you provide a feasible direction with objective tending to infinity?","For minimization, did you check all boundary vertices or supply an equivalent lower-bound proof?","Did changing objective sense leave the feasible region unchanged?"]},{"id":"hw2-claims-staffing","title":"Claims processing: formulate and solve a staffing LP","lectureIds":["linear-programming"],"topicIds":["lp-components","graphical"],"kind":"homework","assignment":"Homework 2 · problem 3","sources":["Assignment 2.pdf · page 2, problem 3","New Answer of assignment 2.pdf · pages 2-3, official key with corrected chart labels"],"prompt":"Universal Claims Processors hires permanent and temporary computer operators. The per-operator data are in the table. The company must process at least 450 claims per day, has at most 40 computer workstations, and allows no more than 25 erroneous claims per day. One operator uses one workstation. Minimize daily wage cost. Formulate the continuous LP requested by the assignment; do not add integer restrictions or round a staffing plan before checking its constraints.","tables":[{"caption":"Daily data per operator","headers":["Operator type","Claims processed / day","Erroneous claims / day","Wage ($/day)"],"rows":[["Permanent","16","0.5","64"],["Temporary","12","1.4","42"]]}],"tasks":["(a) Define decision variables with units and formulate the objective, processing requirement, workstation limit, error limit and variable bounds.","(b) Solve graphically. Identify the feasible polygon, compare its vertices and report the minimum-cost staffing plan and daily cost.","Substitute the chosen plan into all constraints and identify which limits bind."],"hints":[{"title":"Choose the units before the inequalities","body":"Let the variables count operators of each type. Multiply their per-operator rates by operator quantities. Distinguish the processing minimum from the workstation and error maxima."},{"title":"Intersect all three restrictions","body":"The feasible side is above the processing requirement but below the workstation and error limits. Check axis endpoints as well as intersections of row boundaries."},{"title":"Compare costs, then check rounding","body":"Find the intersection of the processing and error boundaries and compare it with all other feasible vertices. Keep full precision to avoid changing feasibility or incorrectly rounding the final cost."}],"solution":{"label":"Official key with corrections","body":"The official key identifies the correct optimal corner. Its workstation/error corner labels and cost rounding require the corrections below.","sections":[{"title":"(a) Formulation","body":"Let x1 be permanent operators and x2 temporary operators, measured as continuous operator quantities.\n$$\\min Z=64x_1+42x_2.$$\n$$16x_1+12x_2\\ge450,\\qquad x_1+x_2\\le40,\\qquad0.5x_1+1.4x_2\\le25,\\qquad x_1,x_2\\ge0.$$"},{"title":"(b) Vertices and minimum","body":"The feasible vertices are (225/8,0), (40,0), (310/9,50/9) and (825/41,875/82). Compare their costs in the table.\nProcessing and errors bind at the optimum. Solving 16x1 + 12x2 = 450 and 0.5x1 + 1.4x2 = 25 gives x1 = 825/41 ≈ 20.121951 and x2 = 875/82 ≈ 10.670732. Cost is 71175/41 ≈ $1,735.975610 per day, which rounds to $1,735.98.\nTotal staffing is 2525/82 ≈ 30.792683, below 40, leaving about 9.207317 workstations unused. The plan processes exactly 450 claims and produces exactly 25 expected erroneous claims per day. An integer-hiring variant would require a separate model and solve."},{"title":"Corrections to the official chart","body":"On key page 3, the workstation/error corner has coordinates (310/9,50/9) ≈ (34.444444,5.555556), not the swapped (5.55,34.45) labels. Its exact cost is 21940/9 ≈ $2,437.777778, or $2,437.78 after rounding, rather than the displayed $2,437.9. The optimal cost rounds to $1,735.98, not $1,735.97. Direct substitution in the printed model verifies these corrections."}],"tables":[{"caption":"Corrected vertex comparison","headers":["Permanent x1","Temporary x2","Daily cost ($)"],"rows":[["28.125","0","1800.000000"],["40","0","2560.000000"],["34.444444","5.555556","2437.777778"],["20.121951","10.670732","1735.975610"]]}],"visual":{"topicId":"a2-claims"}},"compare":["Did you use ≥ for claims processed, and ≤ for workstations and errors?","Are the objective and every constraint expressed per day with consistent units?","Did you compare all four feasible vertices and keep the operator types in the correct coordinate order?","Does your continuous optimum meet processing and error limits exactly while leaving workstation slack?","Did you round the cost only at the end, without rounding the staffing plan to integers?"]},{"id":"hw2-triangle-objectives","title":"Graphical LP: maximum and minimum on one triangle","lectureIds":["linear-programming"],"topicIds":["graphical","irregular"],"kind":"homework","assignment":"Homework 2 · problem 4","sources":["Assignment 2.pdf · page 3, problem 4","New Answer of assignment 2.pdf · pages 3-4, official key"],"prompt":"Variables are continuous and nonnegative. Keep the same constraints in both parts:\n$$Z=800x_1+900x_2.$$\n$$2x_1+4x_2\\le30,\\qquad4x_1+2x_2\\le30,\\qquad x_1+x_2\\ge9,\\qquad x_1,x_2\\ge0.$$","tables":[],"tasks":["(a) Maximize Z using graphical analysis, showing the feasible region and objective comparison.","(b) Minimize Z using the same graphical feasible region.","Report both optimal points and objective values. Identify the active constraints and explain why reversing the objective does not change feasibility."],"hints":[{"title":"Notice the minimum-total requirement","body":"The first two rows are upper bounds, but x1 + x2 ≥ 9 keeps the side away from the origin. Shade their intersection, not the entire region below the two upper bounds."},{"title":"Find the three pairwise row intersections","body":"Intersect each pair of row boundaries and verify nonnegativity and the third inequality. Axis intercepts are not automatically feasible corners."},{"title":"Evaluate one list twice","body":"Evaluate 800x1 + 900x2 at each feasible vertex. Select the largest for (a) and the smallest for (b); do not change the constraints when reversing the objective."}],"solution":{"label":"Derived worked solution","body":"Both results agree with the official key. The same three feasible vertices support the maximization and minimization comparisons.","sections":[{"title":"Feasible vertices","body":"The intersections of the three boundary pairs are (3,6), (5,5) and (6,3). Each satisfies the third row and nonnegativity. Their objective values are $7,800, $8,500 and $7,500, respectively."},{"title":"Maximum and minimum","body":"The maximum is attained at (5,5), with Z = $8,500. Both upper-bound resource rows bind, while x1 + x2 = 10 exceeds the minimum 9 by one.\nThe minimum is attained at (6,3), with Z = $7,500. Here 4x1 + 2x2 = 30 and x1 + x2 = 9 bind. The other row uses 2(6) + 4(3) = 24, leaving slack 6. Reversing the objective sense changes which feasible corner is preferred, while every feasible point continues to satisfy exactly the same constraints."}],"tables":[{"caption":"Objective comparison on the common triangle","headers":["Vertex","Objective Z ($)"],"rows":[["(3,6)","7800"],["(5,5)","8500"],["(6,3)","7500"]]}],"visual":{"topicId":"a2-graphical","figure":2}},"compare":["Did you shade above x1 + x2 = 9 while staying below both resource lines?","Did you find and verify all three feasible vertices?","Did you use the same feasible region for maximization and minimization?","Do your chosen extrema and binding-row checks agree with direct substitution?"]},{"id":"hw3-aluminum","title":"United Aluminum: operating days and cost sensitivity","lectureIds":["sensitivity"],"topicIds":["a3-mills","objective-range","reports"],"kind":"homework","assignment":"Homework 3 · problem 1","sources":["Assignment3.pdf · pages 1-2 (official problem 1 and shared instructions)","Assignment3_Answer.pdf · pages 1-2 (official answer)"],"prompt":"Two mills produce high-, medium-, and low-grade aluminum at the daily rates in the table. The contract requires at least the stated tonnage of every grade. Mill 1 costs $6,000 per operating day; Mill 2 costs $7,000. Choose nonnegative operating days to meet the contract at minimum total cost. This is the continuous LP in Homework 3; complete all four parts.","tables":[{"caption":"Production and contract data","headers":["Grade","Mill 1 (tons/day)","Mill 2 (tons/day)","Minimum contract (tons)"],"rows":[["High",6,2,12],["Medium",2,2,8],["Low",4,10,5]]}],"tasks":["(a) Define the variables and formulate the LP. Draw the feasible region, identify every finite extreme point, evaluate its cost, and find the optimal operating plan.","(b) Compare slopes graphically to find the ranges of c₁ and c₂, changing one cost at a time while holding the other at its original value. State both allowable increases and decreases.","(c) Introduce surplus/slack variables to write equality rows. At your optimal vertex, express the basic variables and Z in terms of the nonbasic variables. Use the nonbasic objective coefficients to justify optimality and derive the coefficient ranges algebraically.","(d) Solve your continuous LP in Excel Solver using Simplex LP and nonnegative decision variables. Generate a Sensitivity Report and compare its optimum, variable reduced costs, and one-at-a-time coefficient ranges with parts (b)–(c). Keep the actual report from your own run; the comparison solution contains expected values, not a claimed Excel run."],"hints":[{"title":"Start with the decision","body":"Use one variable for each mill’s operating days. A contractual minimum requires a greater-than-or-equal production constraint, not an equality forcing exact production."},{"title":"Graph before optimizing","body":"Find the axis intercepts and intersections of the grade boundaries, then retain only points satisfying every requirement. An unbounded feasible region can still have a finite minimum."},{"title":"Make the sensitivity test algebraic","body":"Subtract a nonnegative surplus from each greater-than-or-equal row. Express Z using variables that are zero at the chosen vertex; for minimization their coefficients must not become negative."}],"solution":{"label":"Official key with derived verification","body":"The analytical comparison follows the official answer key, with the clarification stated below where needed. Excel report values are independently derived expectations; no actual Excel run or report is claimed.","sections":[{"title":"(a) Formulation and vertices","body":"Let x1,x2 be operating days.\n$$\\min Z=6000x_1+7000x_2;\\quad6x_1+2x_2\\ge12,\\quad2x_1+2x_2\\ge8,\\quad4x_1+10x_2\\ge5,\\quad x_1,x_2\\ge0.$$\nThe low-grade row is redundant. The unbounded feasible region lies above the high- and medium-grade boundaries. Vertices (0,6), (1,3), and (4,0) cost $42,000, $27,000, and $24,000. The minimum is four days at Mill 1 and zero at Mill 2."},{"title":"(b)-(c) Graphical and algebraic sensitivity","body":"The objective slope is -c1/c2. At (4,0), adjacent boundary slopes are -1 and 0, so 0 <= c1 <= c2 keeps this corner optimal. With c2 = 7,000, c1 ranges from 0 to 7,000 (allowable decrease 6,000; increase 1,000). With c1 = 6,000, c2 ranges from 6,000 to infinity (decrease 1,000; unlimited increase).\nIntroduce surplus variables e1,e2,e3 by subtracting them from the three >= rows. The nonbasic variables are x2 and e2.\n$$x_1=4-x_2+\\tfrac12e_2,\\quad e_1=12-4x_2+3e_2,\\quad e_3=11+6x_2+2e_2.$$\nAt the original costs:\n$$Z=24000+1000x_2+3000e_2.$$\nWith general cost coefficients:\n$$Z=4c_1+(c_2-c_1)x_2+\\tfrac12c_1e_2.$$\nFor minimization, both nonbasic coefficients must remain nonnegative, giving exactly 0 <= c1 <= c2. At the original costs they are strictly positive, proving the unique optimum. Range endpoints can have multiple optima."},{"title":"(d) Solver verification","body":"For part (d), enter decision cells, a SUMPRODUCT objective and constraint left-hand sides, choose Simplex LP and nonnegative variables, then request the Sensitivity Report. Compare its solution and coefficient ranges with the values here. The official key provides the analytical solution but no actual Excel report; the report checks below are independently derived expectations, not a claim that Excel was run. Expected original-row shadow prices (high, medium, low) are ($0,$3,000,$0) per ton. The variable reduced costs are (0,$1,000). Medium demand can decrease from 8 to 4 with the same basis and has no finite upper bound; the high and low rows have current surpluses 12 and 11."}],"tables":[{"caption":"Expected variable part of the Solver Sensitivity Report (derived)","headers":["Variable","Final value","Reduced cost","Coefficient","Allowable increase","Allowable decrease"],"rows":[["Mill 1 days",4,0,6000,1000,6000],["Mill 2 days",0,1000,7000,"Unbounded",1000]]}],"visual":{"topicId":"a3-mills"}},"compare":["Did you distinguish an unbounded feasible region from an unbounded objective?","Do all three production requirements hold at every vertex you keep?","Do the graphical slope bounds and algebraic nonbasic-coefficient signs give the same ranges?","Are your Excel variable reduced costs consistent with the analytical nonbasic-variable calculation?","Did you discuss ties at coefficient-range endpoints rather than claiming uniqueness throughout the closed range?"]},{"id":"hw3-bradley","title":"Bradley farm: acreage, profit ranges and the all-corn threshold","lectureIds":["sensitivity"],"topicIds":["a3-bradley","objective-range","reports"],"kind":"homework","assignment":"Homework 3 · problem 2","sources":["Assignment3.pdf · page 2 (official problem 2)","Assignment3_Answer.pdf · pages 3-5 (official answer)"],"prompt":"The Bradley family has 410 acres and a $52,500 planting budget. Corn costs $105 per acre and earns $300 profit per acre. Tobacco costs $210 per acre and earns $520 profit per acre. Tobacco acreage is limited to 100. Choose nonnegative crop acreages to maximize total profit; unused land and budget are allowed.","tables":[{"caption":"Crop data","headers":["Crop","Cost ($/acre)","Profit ($/acre)","Additional limit"],"rows":[["Corn",105,300,"None"],["Tobacco",210,520,"At most 100 acres"]]}],"tasks":["(a) Formulate the LP, graph the feasible region, label all its corner points, evaluate profit, and choose the maximizing allocation.","(b) Use objective-line rotation to find one-at-a-time sensitivity ranges for corn profit c₁ and tobacco profit c₂. With tobacco profit fixed, find the minimum corn profit at which planting only corn is optimal; distinguish “optimal” from “uniquely optimal.”","(c) Introduce slack variables, express the decision variables and Z in terms of the nonbasic slacks at the optimum, and justify optimality from their reduced-cost signs. Derive the coefficient ranges algebraically. Keep track of whether the budget row is in dollars or has been rescaled.","(d) Solve your continuous LP in Excel Solver using Simplex LP and nonnegative decision variables. Generate a Sensitivity Report and compare its optimum, variable reduced costs, and one-at-a-time coefficient ranges with parts (b)–(c). Keep the actual report from your own run; the comparison solution contains expected values, not a claimed Excel run."],"hints":[{"title":"Separate the resources","body":"Use one acreage variable per crop. Land, planting money, and the tobacco quota are three different upper bounds."},{"title":"Keep units visible","body":"Dividing the budget row by 105 simplifies the graph. If you rescale that row, its slack and any associated objective coefficient are no longer measured in single dollars."},{"title":"Test the corner, then the edge","body":"A coefficient-range endpoint can make the objective parallel to an entire feasible edge. Check whether an all-corn corner is the only best point or one of several best points."}],"solution":{"label":"Official key with corrections","body":"The analytical comparison follows the official answer key, with the clarification stated below where needed. Excel report values are independently derived expectations; no actual Excel run or report is claimed. The official key’s “corn >= $520” condition means all-corn is an optimal plan, not necessarily the only optimal plan. Uniqueness requires a strict increase above $520.","sections":[{"title":"(a) Model and corner points","body":"Let x1 be acres of corn and x2 acres of tobacco.\n$$\\max Z=300x_1+520x_2;\\quad x_1+x_2\\le410,\\quad105x_1+210x_2\\le52500,\\quad x_2\\le100,\\quad x_1,x_2\\ge0.$$\nThe budget row is equivalently x1 + 2x2 <= 500. Vertices (0,0), (0,100), (300,100), (320,90), (410,0) yield profits $0, $52,000, $142,000, $142,800, $123,000. The optimum is (320,90), with land and budget binding and ten acres of unused tobacco quota."},{"title":"(b) Graphical sensitivity and the all-corn threshold","body":"The binding boundary slopes are -1 (land) and -1/2 (budget). Optimality requires 0.5 <= c1/c2 <= 1. With tobacco profit fixed at 520, corn profit ranges from $260 to $520 (decrease 40; increase 220). With corn fixed at 300, tobacco ranges from $300 to $600 (decrease 220; increase 80). An all-corn plan (410,0) first becomes optimal at corn profit $520. At exactly $520 it shares the optimum with a whole land-binding edge; corn profit strictly above $520 makes all-corn uniquely optimal."},{"title":"(c) Algebraic sensitivity","body":"Use x1 + x2 + sL = 410 and x1 + 2x2 + sB = 500. The quota row is x2 + sT = 100.\n$$x_1=320-2s_L+s_B,\\quad x_2=90+s_L-s_B,\\quad s_T=10-s_L+s_B.$$\n$$Z=142800-80s_L-220s_B.$$\nFor general profits the two coefficients are -2c1 + c2 and c1 - c2. Requiring both to be <= 0 gives 0.5c2 <= c1 <= c2, the graphical range. The budget slack sB is measured in units of $105, so its coefficient -220 is not the dollar-denominated shadow price of the original budget."},{"title":"(d) Solver verification","body":"For part (d), enter decision cells, a SUMPRODUCT objective and constraint left-hand sides, choose Simplex LP and nonnegative variables, then request the Sensitivity Report. Compare its solution and coefficient ranges with the values here. The official key provides the analytical solution but no actual Excel report; the report checks below are independently derived expectations, not a claim that Excel was run. Expected original-row shadow prices are $80/acre for land, $220/105 = $2.095238 per budget dollar, and zero for tobacco quota. The current land RHS range is 400 to 500 acres; the original dollar-budget RHS range is $43,050 to $53,550; the quota remains nonbinding for a limit of at least 90 acres."}],"tables":[{"caption":"Expected variable part of the Solver Sensitivity Report (derived)","headers":["Variable","Final acres","Reduced cost","Coefficient","Allowable increase","Allowable decrease"],"rows":[["Corn",320,0,300,220,40],["Tobacco",90,0,520,80,220]]}],"visual":{"topicId":"a3-bradley"}},"compare":["Did you include every land, budget, quota, and nonnegativity restriction?","Are your two binding resource rows and remaining slack consistent with the proposed optimum?","Did the algebra use the same scale for the budget row throughout?","At the all-corn threshold, did you identify alternate optima?","Does your actual Excel report agree with the one-at-a-time ranges, and are dollar-denominated shadow prices distinguished from rescaled-row values?"]},{"id":"hw3-apex","title":"Apex: graph, slack algebra and profit sensitivity","lectureIds":["sensitivity"],"topicIds":["a3-apex","objective-range","reports"],"kind":"homework","assignment":"Homework 3 · problem 3","sources":["Assignment3.pdf · pages 2-3 (official problem 3)","Assignment3_Answer.pdf · pages 5-7 (official answer)"],"prompt":"Apex Precision Manufacturing produces component A (x₁) and component B (x₂). Its continuous daily LP is given below. Use it to complete the graphical, sensitivity, algebraic, and Excel verification parts.\n$$\\max Z=50x_1+40x_2$$\n$$2x_1+x_2\\le100,\\qquad x_1+2x_2\\le80,\\qquad x_1,x_2\\ge0.$$","tables":[{"caption":"Resources and returns","headers":["Item","Component A","Component B","Available"],"rows":[["Machining units/component",2,1,100],["Finishing units/component",1,2,80],["Profit ($/component)",50,40,"—"]]}],"tasks":["(a) Draw the feasible region, find every extreme point, evaluate Z at each point, and determine the maximizing production mix and daily profit.","(b) Use the slopes of the binding boundaries to find the coefficient range for c₁ with c₂ = 40 and for c₂ with c₁ = 50. State allowable increases and decreases.","(c) Let s₁ and s₂ be machining and finishing slacks. Express x₁, x₂, and Z in terms of the nonbasic slacks at the optimum. Explain optimality using their objective coefficients, then derive both coefficient ranges algebraically.","(d) Solve your continuous LP in Excel Solver using Simplex LP and nonnegative decision variables. Generate a Sensitivity Report and compare its optimum, variable reduced costs, and one-at-a-time coefficient ranges with parts (b)–(c). Keep the actual report from your own run; the comparison solution contains expected values, not a claimed Excel run."],"hints":[{"title":"Construct the feasible polygon","body":"Find each resource line’s intercepts and their intersection, then keep the side containing the origin. Do not assume a line intersection is feasible until you check both nonnegativity bounds."},{"title":"Separate geometry and algebra","body":"The objective-line slope must lie between the two active boundary slopes. For the algebraic version, solve the two equality rows for the decision variables in terms of the slacks."},{"title":"Use the maximization sign test","body":"At a slack-zero vertex, increasing any nonbasic slack cannot improve a maximum. Require its coefficient in Z to remain nonpositive; equality can create multiple optima."}],"solution":{"label":"Official key with derived verification","body":"The analytical comparison follows the official answer key, with the clarification stated below where needed. Excel report values are independently derived expectations; no actual Excel run or report is claimed.","sections":[{"title":"(a) Model and graphical solution","body":"$$\\max Z=50x_1+40x_2;\\quad2x_1+x_2\\le100,\\quad x_1+2x_2\\le80,\\quad x_1,x_2\\ge0.$$\nThe feasible polygon has vertices (0,0), (0,40), (40,20), and (50,0). Their profits are $0, $1,600, $2,800, and $2,500. Thus produce 40 A and 20 B for $2,800/day."},{"title":"(b)-(c) Coefficient sensitivity by slopes and slack algebra","body":"Boundary slopes at the optimum are -2 and -1/2. Thus 0.5 <= c1/c2 <= 2. With c2 = 40, c1 ranges from 20 to 80; with c1 = 50, c2 ranges from 25 to 100. Allowable changes are +/-30 for c1, and decrease 15 / increase 60 for c2.\nIntroduce machining slack s1 and finishing slack s2:\n$$x_1=40-\\tfrac23s_1+\\tfrac13s_2,\\quad x_2=20+\\tfrac13s_1-\\tfrac23s_2,\\quad Z=2800-20s_1-10s_2.$$\nFor general c1,c2, the slack coefficients are (-2c1 + c2)/3 and (c1 - 2c2)/3. Both must stay <= 0 for maximization, reproducing 0.5c2 <= c1 <= 2c2. Negative coefficients at the stated profits justify optimality."},{"title":"(d) Solver verification","body":"For part (d), enter decision cells, a SUMPRODUCT objective and constraint left-hand sides, choose Simplex LP and nonnegative variables, then request the Sensitivity Report. Compare its solution and coefficient ranges with the values here. The official key provides the analytical solution but no actual Excel report; the report checks below are independently derived expectations, not a claim that Excel was run. Expected shadow prices are $20 per machining unit and $10 per finishing unit; both decision-variable reduced costs are zero. Holding the other RHS fixed, the same-basis machining-capacity range is 40 to 160, and finishing-capacity range is 50 to 200."}],"tables":[{"caption":"Expected variable part of the Solver Sensitivity Report (derived)","headers":["Variable","Final units","Reduced cost","Coefficient","Allowable increase","Allowable decrease"],"rows":[["Component A",40,0,50,30,30],["Component B",20,0,40,60,15]]}],"visual":{"topicId":"a3-apex"}},"compare":["Did you evaluate the objective at all feasible vertices, including the origin and axis corners?","Are the variable formulas obtained from both equality rows rather than from only one boundary?","Do the slack-objective coefficients have the correct signs for maximization?","Do graphical and algebraic ranges agree, including endpoint ties?","Did you distinguish decision-variable reduced costs from the objective coefficients of nonbasic slacks and from row shadow prices?"]},{"id":"solver-pottery-lp","title":"Pottery: write, inspect and solve an LP file","lectureIds":["computer-solution"],"topicIds":["copt","copt-files","copt-inspection"],"kind":"lecture","assignment":"Practice based on the COPT tutorial","sources":["copt 26-09-29.pptx · slides 4–10","chapter03-2--5beb6393ab.pdf · pages 4–7"],"prompt":"Recreate the lecture’s continuous pottery example from the production data. All available labor and clay are upper bounds. This revisits the demonstrated example; it is not an additional assigned homework question. Use the physical clay coefficients in the table: 4 lb per bowl and 3 lb per mug.","tables":[{"caption":"Pottery production data","headers":["Product","Labor (hours/unit)","Clay (lb/unit)","Profit ($/unit)"],"rows":[["Bowl",1,4,40],["Mug",2,3,50],["Available",40,120,"—"]]}],"tasks":["Write a plain-text pottery.lp file with named objective and constraint rows, explicit nonnegative bounds, and an End marker.","Load it in the COPT shell. Inspect its row count, column count, nonzero matrix-element count, and objective sense before solving.","Export the loaded model to LP or MPS and compare every coefficient, sign, RHS, and bound with the intended model.","Solve, check the final status, export the solution, and substitute the returned quantities into both physical resource constraints. Report quantities, profit, usages, and slacks."],"hints":[{"title":"Organize the file","body":"Separate objective direction, objective, constraint rows, and bounds. Use plain text, unique row names, and spaces between numeric coefficients and variable names."},{"title":"Inspect what was loaded","body":"A syntactically accepted file need not encode the model you intended. Compare the loaded columns and row coefficients with the source data before interpreting the result."},{"title":"Validate an optimum","body":"Read the status before the objective. After solving, independently calculate labor, clay, profit, and resource slack from the returned variables."}],"solution":{"label":"Derived worked solution","body":"This reproduces the corrected tutorial example. The named rows and filenames below are readable adaptations of the demonstrated workflow.","sections":[{"title":"Expected model inspection and optimum","body":"The loaded model has 2 algebraic constraint rows, 2 variables, and 4 nonzero matrix coefficients; maximization is the objective sense. The optimum is 24 bowls and 8 mugs, with profit $1,360. Labor usage is 24 + 2(8) = 40 hours and clay usage is 4(24) + 3(8) = 120 lb; both slacks are zero. Bounds are not counted as extra algebraic rows."}],"code":[{"title":"pottery.lp","language":"text","body":"Maximize\n  profit: 40 x1 + 50 x2\nSubject To\n  labor: x1 + 2 x2 <= 40\n  clay: 4 x1 + 3 x2 <= 120\nBounds\n  x1 >= 0\n  x2 >= 0\nEnd"},{"title":"COPT shell workflow","language":"text","body":"read pottery.lp\nget rows\nget cols\nget elems\nget objsense\nwritelp pottery-check.lp\nwritemps pottery.mps\nopt\nwritesol pottery.sol"}],"downloads":[{"title":"Download the corrected pottery LP","href":"assets/examples/pottery.lp"}],"visual":{"model":"pottery"}},"compare":["Does the exported loaded model match the production data, not merely the input text?","Did you check all dimensions and all four matrix coefficients?","Does the final solver status support calling the solution optimal?","Did you compute both resource usages independently and retain their units?"]},{"id":"solver-pottery-diagnosis","title":"Debug a solved model that gives the wrong business answer","lectureIds":["computer-solution"],"topicIds":["copt","copt-files","copt-inspection"],"kind":"extra","assignment":"Additional practice based on a displayed lecture typo","sources":["chapter03-2--5beb6393ab.pdf · pages 25–27 (mistyped pottery screenshot)","copt 26-09-29.pptx · slides 4–9 (corrected model and inspection)"],"prompt":"A colleague used the correct pottery data in the table but typed the following model. The solver accepts it and reports an optimum. Explain why that alone does not validate the business formulation. This is an additional diagnostic exercise created for the study companion, based on the typo visible in the older course screenshot.\n$$\\max Z=40x_1+50x_2$$\n$$x_1+2x_2\\le40,\\qquad4x_2+3x_2\\le120,\\qquad x_1,x_2\\ge0.$$","tables":[{"caption":"Pottery production data","headers":["Product","Labor (hours/unit)","Clay (lb/unit)","Profit ($/unit)"],"rows":[["Bowl",1,4,40],["Mug",2,3,50],["Available",40,120,"—"]]}],"tasks":["Compare the typed clay row with the physical production data. Explain which product is missing from that row.","Simplify the typed row, determine the resulting loaded matrix’s number of nonzero elements, and solve or reason through this typed model.","Test the typed model’s returned plan against the actual clay requirement. State whether it is physically feasible.","Correct the model, solve it again, and explain why both a wrong model and a corrected model can receive an Optimal status."],"hints":[{"title":"Read the symbols, not the intended meaning","body":"Identify which variable each coefficient multiplies. A solver optimizes the supplied algebra and cannot infer a product’s physical resource use."},{"title":"Simplify before comparing","body":"Repeated terms on the same variable combine. Compare the resulting coefficient row with the two entries in the production table."},{"title":"Use an independent physical check","body":"Evaluate actual clay as 4 times bowls plus 3 times mugs, regardless of the row appearing in the typed file."}],"solution":{"label":"Derived worked solution","body":"The typed row is 7x₂ ≤ 120: it contains no x₁ coefficient, so bowls incorrectly use zero clay in that model. The loaded matrix has three nonzero coefficients, not four.","sections":[{"title":"Why the wrong model returns a larger objective","body":"Under the typed model, bowls return $40 per labor hour and mugs $25 per labor hour. The optimum is (40,0), profit $1,600. It satisfies the typed clay row but physically consumes 4(40) = 160 lb, exceeding the actual 120 lb supply by 40 lb."},{"title":"Repair and compare","body":"The correct clay row is 4x₁ + 3x₂ ≤ 120. Its optimum is (24,8), profit $1,360, with both resource constraints binding. Optimal status is a statement about the supplied formulation, not a certificate that its coefficients describe the business problem. This coefficient error is distinct from the tutorial’s separate parser example in which an unspaced token such as 4x2 becomes a variable name."}],"visual":{"model":"pottery"}},"compare":["Did you identify the omitted bowl coefficient rather than changing the clay RHS?","Does your wrong-model optimum satisfy its written rows?","Did you separately check the original physical clay calculation?","Does the repaired model match the table and the corrected tutorial example?"]},{"id":"solver-pottery-report","title":"Interpret the pottery Sensitivity Report","lectureIds":["computer-solution","sensitivity"],"topicIds":["reports","objective-range","labor-shadow","clay-shadow"],"kind":"lecture","assignment":"Practice based on the lecture’s sensitivity report","sources":["chapter03-2--5beb6393ab.pdf · pages 10–11","chapter02 - part 2.pdf · pages 26–38"],"prompt":"Use the following entries transcribed from the lecture’s pottery Sensitivity Report. Treat each proposed parameter change separately, holding all other model data fixed. Derive conclusions from the report rather than reading an unchanged variable mix as unchanged numerical production quantities.","tables":[{"caption":"Objective coefficients from the displayed report","headers":["Variable","Current coefficient","Allowable increase","Allowable decrease"],"rows":[["Bowls",40,"26.6666667",15],["Mugs",50,30,20]]},{"caption":"Constraint entries from the displayed report","headers":["Resource","Current RHS","Shadow price","Allowable increase","Allowable decrease"],"rows":[["Labor (hours)",40,16,40,10],["Clay (lb)",120,6,40,60]]}],"tasks":["Compute each one-at-a-time objective-coefficient interval. Explain what may happen at an interval endpoint.","Compute the same-basis labor and clay RHS intervals. Explain whether production quantities must stay numerically fixed within them.","Predict the profit change when labor rises from 40 to 60 hours. Separately predict the change when clay falls from 120 to 80 lb.","Explain why the report alone cannot justify simultaneously applying both changes or extending one shadow price beyond its allowable RHS range."],"hints":[{"title":"Turn allowable changes into endpoints","body":"An interval runs from current value minus allowable decrease to current value plus allowable increase. Keep objective-coefficient ranges separate from RHS ranges."},{"title":"Use units in the prediction","body":"Within an applicable one-at-a-time RHS interval, multiply the RHS change by its shadow price. A decrease is a negative change."},{"title":"State exactly what is preserved","body":"A retained optimal basis can produce changed quantities. At an endpoint another optimum or a degenerate basic solution may arise; outside the interval re-solve rather than extrapolate."}],"solution":{"label":"Derived worked solution","body":"Bowl profit lies from $25 to $200/3 ≈ $66.67; mug profit lies from $30 to $80. The displayed increase 26.6666667 is a rounded 80/3; adding it to 40 gives the exact upper endpoint 200/3. An adjacent edge can tie at an objective-range endpoint.","sections":[{"title":"RHS intervals and predicted changes","body":"Labor can range from 30 to 80 hours; clay from 60 to 160 lb with the current basis. Labor rising alone by 20 hours changes profit by 20($16) = +$320. Clay falling alone by 40 lb changes profit by −40($6) = −$240."},{"title":"What the report does not say","body":"The original quantities (24,8) need not remain numerically fixed: with labor alone at 60 the solution is (12,24), while with clay alone at 80 it is (8,16). These two plans describe separate changed models. Combining individually admissible changes can leave the original basis’s feasible region, so one-at-a-time intervals do not automatically authorize simultaneous changes. The lecture’s “unchanged solution variable mix” is interpreted as the basis/product pattern, not fixed quantities."}]},"compare":["Did you subtract decreases and add increases to the correct current values?","Were endpoint ties distinguished from a unique optimum?","Did you retain the signs and units of the two resource changes?","Did you avoid applying a one-at-a-time report as an independent box of simultaneous valid changes?"]},{"id":"solver-pottery-python","title":"Build and verify the pottery LP with coptpy","lectureIds":["computer-solution"],"topicIds":["copt-python","reports"],"kind":"lecture","assignment":"Practice based on the COPT Python tutorial","sources":["copt 26-09-29.pptx · slide 11","chapter03-2--5beb6393ab.pdf · pages 4–7,10"],"prompt":"Rebuild the corrected continuous pottery LP directly with the COPT Python API using the table. Use nonnegative variables and named resource constraints. The programming exercise revisits the tutorial; it is not a new homework requirement. Follow the course’s COPT installation/setup instructions before running it.","tables":[{"caption":"Pottery production data","headers":["Product","Labor (hours/unit)","Clay (lb/unit)","Profit ($/unit)"],"rows":[["Bowl",1,4,40],["Mug",2,3,50],["Available",40,120,"—"]]}],"tasks":["Create the environment and model, add variables and both resource constraints, set the maximizing profit objective, and solve.","Check the final model status before reporting an optimum. Print the objective and variable values.","Print variable reduced costs and row dual values for this continuous LP. Interpret their units without treating them as unconditional global resource values.","Independently recompute resource usage and profit, and compare the output with the corrected LP-file formulation."],"hints":[{"title":"Translate each model component once","body":"Use variables for the decisions, one constraint for each physical resource, and one objective built from profit coefficients. Keep model creation separate from result interpretation."},{"title":"Guard the result","body":"Use COPT.OPTIMAL before calling a result optimal. Other statuses need their own interpretation rather than blindly reading an objective."},{"title":"Check meanings as well as numbers","body":"A variable’s reduced cost and a constraint’s dual value answer different questions. Their sensitivity interpretation here belongs to the continuous LP and its current solution basis."}],"solution":{"label":"Derived worked solution","body":"The program below follows tutorial slide 11, using named constraints and an explicit non-optimal-status guard. It returns 24 bowls, 8 mugs, objective 1,360, variable reduced costs zero, and labor/clay duals 16 and 6. These resource duals are local dollars per labor hour and dollars per pound of clay. A printed reduced cost of -0 is numerical zero.","sections":[{"title":"Independent substitution","body":"Profit is 40(24) + 50(8) = 1,360; labor is 24 + 2(8) = 40; clay is 4(24) + 3(8) = 120. Both slacks are zero. Dual-resource valuation agrees with product profits: 16 + 4(6) = 40 and 2(16) + 3(6) = 50. These are checks of the continuous LP, not a sensitivity report for an integer production model."}],"code":[{"title":"Complete pottery example (requires coptpy)","language":"python","body":"\"\"\"Pottery LP from the COPT course tutorial. Requires coptpy.\"\"\"\nimport coptpy as cp\nfrom coptpy import COPT\n\nenv = cp.Envr()\nmodel = env.createModel(\"pottery\")\nx1 = model.addVar(lb=0, name=\"x1\")\nx2 = model.addVar(lb=0, name=\"x2\")\nmodel.addConstr(x1 + 2*x2 <= 40, name=\"labor\")\nmodel.addConstr(4*x1 + 3*x2 <= 120, name=\"clay\")\nmodel.setObjective(40*x1 + 50*x2, sense=COPT.MAXIMIZE)\nmodel.setParam(COPT.Param.TimeLimit, 10.0)\nmodel.solve()\nif model.status != COPT.OPTIMAL:\n    raise RuntimeError(f\"No optimal solution: status {model.status}\")\nprint(f\"Objective: {model.objval:g}\")\nfor var in model.getVars():\n    print(f\"{var.name}: value={var.x:g}, reduced_cost={var.RC:g}\")\nfor con in model.getConstrs():\n    print(f\"{con.name}: dual={con.pi:g}\")\n"}],"downloads":[{"title":"Download the complete pottery Python example","href":"assets/examples/copt-pottery.py"}],"visual":{"model":"pottery"}},"compare":["Did you include both resource rows with the correct coefficients?","Was Optimal status checked before interpreting objective and solution values?","Do the independent physical calculations agree with the solver output?","Are reduced costs and row duals interpreted separately with the right units and local-range limitation?"]},{"id":"practice-hw4-transport","title":"Engine transport with two prohibited lanes","lectureIds":["modeling-examples"],"topicIds":["transport"],"kind":"homework","assignment":"Homework 4 · part a","sources":["Homework 4.pdf · page 2 (official problem a; derived solution)"],"prompt":"A manufacturer can supply engines monthly from four plants to three trucking firms. Plants 1-4 are Phoenix, Seattle, St. Louis and Detroit. Customers A-C are Greensboro, Charlotte and Louisville. Capacities are upper bounds; customer requirements must be met. Charlotte refuses engines from Seattle, and Louisville refuses engines from Detroit. Costs in the table are in hundreds of dollars per engine. Choose monthly shipments to minimize total transportation cost.","tables":[{"caption":"Monthly engine capacities and unit shipping costs (hundreds of dollars)","headers":["Plant","Capacity (engines/month)","A: Greensboro","B: Charlotte","C: Louisville"],"rows":[["1 Phoenix",5,7,8,5],["2 Seattle",25,6,"10 (route prohibited)",6],["3 St. Louis",20,10,4,5],["4 Detroit",25,3,9,"11 (route prohibited)"]]},{"caption":"Monthly customer requirements","headers":["Customer","A: Greensboro","B: Charlotte","C: Louisville"],"rows":[["Demand (engines)",10,20,15]]}],"tasks":["Define the decision variables, objective and every constraint; give units and identify any modeling assumptions.","Build and solve the model in Excel Solver using the LP method, then report the decision-variable values and objective value.","For the original assignment submission, include the Excel workbook(s) and the PDF answers together in one ZIP. The comparison below does not substitute for an actual workbook or Solver run."],"hints":[{"title":"Choose quantities on lanes","body":"Use one nonnegative shipment variable for each allowed plant-customer lane. A prohibited lane should be omitted or fixed at zero."},{"title":"Keep supply and demand directions distinct","body":"Plant totals cannot exceed capacity. Customer inflows should equal their specified requirements. Compare total capacity with total demand before deciding whether supply rows can be equalities."},{"title":"Check units and shared capacities","body":"Multiply each lane shipment by its own table cost. Convert hundreds of dollars to dollars only after computing the objective; do not allocate every customer independently without checking plant totals."}],"solution":{"label":"Derived worked solution","body":"This is a reader-derived worked solution based on the instructor’s problem data, not an official answer key. Numerical results have been independently checked.","sections":[{"title":"Model","body":"Let xij >= 0 be engines from plant i to customer j. Using the cost table in hundreds of dollars, minimize the sum of cij*xij over allowed arcs. For each plant impose sum_j xij <= its capacity; for each customer impose sum_i xij = its demand. Set x2B = 0 and x4C = 0, or omit those variables entirely. There are no constraints requiring every plant to run at full capacity."},{"title":"One derived optimal plan","body":"Ship five Phoenix engines and ten Seattle engines to Louisville, twenty St. Louis engines to Charlotte, and ten Detroit engines to Greensboro. All other arcs carry zero. Cost is 5(5) + 6(10) + 4(20) + 3(10) = 195 hundred dollars = $19,500. Demand totals only 45 against total capacity 75; adding fictitious real-customer demand or forcing equality on every plant would change the problem."},{"title":"Complete objective and constraint structure","body":"For plants i = 1,...,4 and customers j = A,B,C, let xij be engines shipped. Use the source-table costs cij.\n$$\\min Z=\\sum_{i=1}^{4}\\sum_{j\\in\\{A,B,C\\}}c_{ij}x_{ij}.$$\n$$\\sum_jx_{1j}\\le5,\\quad\\sum_jx_{2j}\\le25,\\quad\\sum_jx_{3j}\\le20,\\quad\\sum_jx_{4j}\\le25.$$\n$$\\sum_ix_{iA}=10,\\quad\\sum_ix_{iB}=20,\\quad\\sum_ix_{iC}=15,\\quad x_{2B}=x_{4C}=0,\\quad x_{ij}\\ge0.$$\nZ is in hundreds of dollars. The displayed optimal flow costs 195, hence $19,500."}],"tables":[{"caption":"One optimal shipment plan (engines/month)","headers":["Plant","Greensboro","Charlotte","Louisville"],"rows":[["Phoenix","0","0","5"],["Seattle","0","0","10"],["St. Louis","0","20","0"],["Detroit","10","0","0"]]}]},"compare":["Did you prohibit Seattle-to-Charlotte and Detroit-to-Louisville, rather than merely give them high costs?","Are plant rows upper bounds and customer rows exact requirements?","Does your plan meet all three demands without exceeding a plant capacity?","Is your objective expressed consistently in hundreds of dollars or converted to dollars?","A different optimal lane allocation can be accepted if it is feasible and has the same minimum objective; retain your actual Excel workbook."]},{"id":"practice-hw4-byproduct","title":"Production with sale and disposal of a by-product","lectureIds":["modeling-examples"],"topicIds":["shirts","blending","pet-food"],"kind":"homework","assignment":"Homework 4 · part b","sources":["Homework 4.pdf · page 2 (official problem b; derived solution)"],"prompt":"A factory produces A and B through two processes. Each ton of B also generates two tons of by-product C. Up to five tons of C can be sold; all remaining C must be discarded. A and B have stated profits of 400 and 800 yuan per ton. Each ton of C sold earns 300 yuan, while each ton discarded costs 200 yuan. Choose production, by-product sales and disposal to maximize total profit. Use the processing requirements and capacities below.","tables":[{"caption":"Processing hours per ton and available hours","headers":["Resource","A (hours/ton)","B (hours/ton)","Available hours"],"rows":[["Process 1",2,3,15],["Process 2",3,4,25]]},{"caption":"By-product facts","headers":["Quantity","Value"],"rows":[["C generated per ton of B","2 tons"],["C sales upper bound","5 tons"],["Profit from C sold","300 yuan/ton"],["Cost of C discarded","200 yuan/ton"]]}],"tasks":["Define the decision variables, objective and every constraint; give units and identify any modeling assumptions.","Build and solve the model in Excel Solver using the LP method, then report the decision-variable values and objective value.","For the original assignment submission, include the Excel workbook(s) and the PDF answers together in one ZIP. The comparison below does not substitute for an actual workbook or Solver run."],"hints":[{"title":"Separate generation from sales","body":"The quantity of C generated is determined by B production. Introduce separate variables for the amounts of C sold and discarded."},{"title":"Account for every ton of C","body":"Write a mass-balance equality between C generation, sales and disposal. The market limit applies to sales, not to generation."},{"title":"Keep the disposal sign negative","body":"Start with the stated profits of A and B, add by-product sale profit, and subtract disposal cost. Check both process-hour limits and nonnegativity."}],"solution":{"label":"Derived worked solution","body":"This is a reader-derived worked solution based on the instructor’s problem data, not an official answer key. Numerical results have been independently checked.","sections":[{"title":"Model","body":"Let a,b be tons of A and B, s the tons of C sold, and d the tons discarded. All are nonnegative.\n$$\\max Z=400a+800b+300s-200d;\\quad2a+3b\\le15,\\quad3a+4b\\le25,\\quad s+d=2b,\\quad s\\le5.$$\nThe by-product balance is an equality: all generated C must be either sold or discarded. The sale cap applies to s, not to total C generation."},{"title":"Derived solution","body":"One optimum is a = 3.75, b = 2.5, s = 5, d = 0, yielding 1,500 + 2,000 + 1,500 = 5,000 yuan. Process 1 is full; Process 2 uses 21.25 of 25 hours. This is a derived numerical check of the formulation, independently solved with HiGHS; it is not an official answer key."},{"title":"Feasibility and an optimality bound","body":"At (a,b,s,d) = (3.75,2.5,5,0), Process 1 uses 15 hours and Process 2 uses 21.25, within the limits. By-product generation is 2b = 5 = s + d. Profit is 400(3.75) + 800(2.5) + 300(5) = 5,000 yuan.\nUsing d = 2b - s, the profit is 400a + 400b + 500s. Since d >= 0 implies s <= 2b, this profit is at most 200(2a + 3b) + 400s <= 200(15) + 400(5) = 5,000. The feasible plan attains that bound."}],"tables":[]},"compare":["Does every ton of B generate exactly two tons of C in your balance?","Is the five-ton upper bound placed on C sold?","Is disposal charged at minus 200 yuan per ton?","Are both process limits present, with the correct A/B coefficients?","Check the solution’s process hours, sale/disposal balance and objective before comparing the optimum."]},{"id":"practice-hw4-farm","title":"Allocate crops with equal cultivation proportions","lectureIds":["modeling-examples"],"topicIds":["shirts"],"kind":"homework","assignment":"Homework 4 · part c","sources":["Homework 4.pdf · page 3 (official problem c; derived solution)"],"prompt":"A 2,000-acre farm is divided among three farm families into plots with the sizes below. Any of corn, peas and soybeans may be planted on any plot. Each plot must have at least 60% of its acreage cultivated, and the same proportion of every plot must be under cultivation. Crop acreage maxima apply across the entire farm. Choose the acreage of each crop on each plot to maximize total profit.","tables":[{"caption":"Plot sizes","headers":["Plot","Acres"],"rows":[["1",500],["2",800],["3",700]]},{"caption":"Farmwide crop limits and profits","headers":["Crop","Maximum acreage","Profit ($/acre)"],"rows":[["Corn",900,600],["Peas",700,450],["Soybeans",1000,300]]}],"tasks":["Define the decision variables, objective and every constraint; give units and identify any modeling assumptions.","Build and solve the model in Excel Solver using the LP method, then report the decision-variable values and objective value.","For the original assignment submission, include the Excel workbook(s) and the PDF answers together in one ZIP. The comparison below does not substitute for an actual workbook or Solver run."],"hints":[{"title":"Use plot-by-crop variables","body":"One variable per crop is insufficient to impose a separate cultivation condition on each plot. Use acres xij of crop j on plot i."},{"title":"Represent a common proportion","body":"Introduce a single cultivation fraction t. A plot’s total cultivated acreage is its fixed acreage multiplied by t; this stays linear because plot size is a constant."},{"title":"Separate plot limits from crop limits","body":"Bound t between 0.6 and 1. Aggregate each crop across all three plots for its crop maximum, and compute profit from planted acres."}],"solution":{"label":"Derived worked solution","body":"This is a reader-derived worked solution based on the instructor’s problem data, not an official answer key. Numerical results have been independently checked.","sections":[{"title":"Model","body":"Let xij >= 0 be acres of crop j on plot i, with j = C,P,S. Let t be the common cultivated fraction and Li the plot acreage.\n$$\\max Z=600\\sum_i x_{iC}+450\\sum_i x_{iP}+300\\sum_i x_{iS}.$$\n$$\\sum_j x_{ij}=L_it\\quad(i=1,2,3),\\qquad0.6\\le t\\le1.$$\n$$\\sum_i x_{iC}\\le900,\\quad\\sum_i x_{iP}\\le700,\\quad\\sum_i x_{iS}\\le1000.$$\nThese equalities are linear because plot sizes Li are constants. Equal absolute acreage on each plot would not represent equal proportions."},{"title":"One derived optimum","body":"Use t = 1, planting 900 total acres of corn, 700 of peas, and 400 of soybeans. The table is one feasible distribution; many equally profitable distributions across plots exist. Profit is 900(600) + 700(450) + 400(300) = $975,000. Every plot is fully cultivated, so the equal-proportion and minimum-cultivation requirements both hold."},{"title":"Why the reported objective is a global maximum","body":"Let C,P,S be total acres of corn, peas and soybeans. Any feasible plan satisfies C + P + S <= 2000, C <= 900 and P <= 700. Thus\n$$600C+450P+300S=300(C+P+S)+300C+150P\\le300(2000)+300(900)+150(700)=975000.$$\nThe displayed plan fills each plot completely, respects all crop maxima and attains $975,000. Other plot allocations with the same total crop acres can attain the same profit."}],"tables":[{"caption":"One optimal crop allocation (acres)","headers":["Plot","Corn","Peas","Soybeans","Cultivated / size"],"rows":[["1","0","500","0","500 / 500"],["2","800","0","0","800 / 800"],["3","100","200","400","700 / 700"]]}]},"compare":["Did you impose equal fractions, rather than equal absolute acres, on the three plots?","Are all cultivated fractions at least 60% and at most 100%?","Do the crop maxima apply to totals across the farm?","Does every plot’s allocation sum to its allowed cultivated acreage?","Plot-level allocations need not match the example: compare feasibility, total crop acreages and maximum profit, because optimal distributions can be nonunique."]},{"id":"practice-hw4-investment","title":"Allocate a coffee shop’s annual investment funds","lectureIds":["modeling-examples"],"topicIds":["portfolio"],"kind":"homework","assignment":"Homework 4 · part d","sources":["Homework 4.pdf · page 3 (official problem d; derived solution)"],"prompt":"A coffee shop has $50,000 to allocate among baking business, supplier contracts, a loyalty program and marketing campaigns. It wants to maximize the next year’s return. Baking can receive no more than 30% of the total investment. Supplier contracts cannot receive more than the other three options combined. At least 20% must go to loyalty, and at least 40% must go to baking and marketing combined. The annual return rates are below.","tables":[{"caption":"Investment alternatives","headers":["Option","Annual return"],"rows":[["Baking business","15%"],["Supplier contracts","8%"],["Loyalty program","6%"],["Marketing campaigns","20%"]]}],"tasks":["Define the decision variables, objective and every constraint; give units and identify any modeling assumptions.","Build and solve the model in Excel Solver using the LP method, then report the decision-variable values and objective value.","For the original assignment submission, include the Excel workbook(s) and the PDF answers together in one ZIP. The comparison below does not substitute for an actual workbook or Solver run."],"hints":[{"title":"Choose dollars allocated","body":"Use one nonnegative dollar-allocation variable per alternative. State whether the entire fixed fund is allocated; the worked model treats the $50,000 as the total allocation."},{"title":"Translate the percentage restrictions","body":"Because the total fund is fixed, convert percentage limits into dollar limits. Keep “no more than” separate from “at least.”"},{"title":"Write the comparison restriction correctly","body":"Supplier investment is compared with the sum of the other three investments. The baking-and-marketing requirement applies to that combined group, not to each member separately."}],"solution":{"label":"Derived worked solution","body":"This is a reader-derived worked solution based on the instructor’s problem data, not an official answer key. Numerical results have been independently checked.","sections":[{"title":"Model","body":"Let x1,x2,x3,x4 be dollars allocated to the four options in that order.\n$$\\max Z=0.15x_1+0.08x_2+0.06x_3+0.20x_4.$$\n$$x_1+x_2+x_3+x_4=50000,\\quad x_1\\le15000,\\quad x_2\\le x_1+x_3+x_4,$$\n$$x_3\\ge10000,\\quad x_1+x_4\\ge20000,\\quad x_1,x_2,x_3,x_4\\ge0.$$\nZ measures annual return, not principal plus return."},{"title":"Derived solution","body":"Invest $10,000 in loyalty and $40,000 in marketing, with zero in baking and supplier contracts. Annual return is $8,600 and year-end principal plus return is $58,600. Marketing dominates the other optional investments; the binding loyalty minimum is the only reason not to allocate everything to marketing. The zero baking allocation respects an upper bound, not a minimum."},{"title":"Feasibility and return bound","body":"The model allocates the entire $50,000. The plan (x1,x2,x3,x4) = (0,0,10000,40000) gives $8,600 annual return and respects every restriction. The supplier and baking allocations may be zero because their restrictions are upper bounds.\nUsing the budget equality, return = 10000 - 0.05x1 - 0.12x2 - 0.14x3. Nonnegativity and x3 >= 10000 imply return <= 10000 - 1400 = 8600. The plan attains this bound. Year-end principal plus return is $58,600."}],"tables":[]},"compare":["Do the four allocations sum to the stated fund?","Did you use the four return rates as decimal coefficients and maximize annual return?","Are the baking upper bound, loyalty lower bound and combined baking/marketing lower bound all present?","Is supplier investment bounded by the other three options combined?","Compare return earned separately from principal plus return, and verify all bounds at your solution."]},{"id":"practice-hw4-inventory","title":"Plan quarterly heater production and inventory","lectureIds":["modeling-examples"],"topicIds":["production"],"kind":"homework","assignment":"Homework 4 · part e","sources":["Homework 4.pdf · pages 3-4 (official problem e; derived solution)"],"prompt":"A heater manufacturer must meet each quarter’s demand on time; backorders are not allowed. Regular production is at most 450 units per quarter at $120 each. Subcontracting is at most 200 units per quarter at $180 each. Inventory holding costs $15 per unit per quarter and is charged on end-of-quarter inventory. Initial inventory and the required inventory after quarter 4 are both zero. Choose regular production, subcontracting and inventory to minimize total cost.","tables":[{"caption":"Demand schedule","headers":["Quarter",1,2,3,4],"rows":[["Demand (units)",580,300,320,700]]}],"tasks":["Define the decision variables, objective and every constraint; give units and identify any modeling assumptions.","Build and solve the model in Excel Solver using the LP method, then report the decision-variable values and objective value.","For the original assignment submission, include the Excel workbook(s) and the PDF answers together in one ZIP. The comparison below does not substitute for an actual workbook or Solver run."],"hints":[{"title":"Carry stock through time","body":"Use a balance equation in every quarter: beginning stock plus both kinds of production equals demand plus ending stock."},{"title":"Apply capacity each quarter","body":"The production and subcontracting caps are per-quarter limits. Do not replace them with only an annual total-capacity condition."},{"title":"Check both boundaries and holding costs","body":"Set the first beginning inventory and the last ending inventory to zero. Use nonnegative inventory to prohibit backorders, and charge holding cost on the correct period’s ending stock."}],"solution":{"label":"Derived worked solution","body":"This is a reader-derived worked solution based on the instructor’s problem data, not an official answer key. Numerical results have been independently checked.","sections":[{"title":"Model","body":"Let rt,st,It be regular production, subcontracted production and end-of-quarter inventory for t = 1,...,4, and dt the demand data.\n$$\\min Z=\\sum_{t=1}^{4}(120r_t+180s_t+15I_t).$$\n$$I_{t-1}+r_t+s_t-I_t=d_t,\\quad0\\le r_t\\le450,\\quad0\\le s_t\\le200,\\quad I_t\\ge0.$$\n$$I_0=0,\\qquad I_4=0.$$\nIncluding 15I4 in the objective is harmless because I4 is fixed at zero. Capacity cannot cover first-quarter demand without subcontracting: regular supply is only 450 against demand 580."},{"title":"Derived optimal schedule","body":"The schedule below meets all demand. Its regular-production cost is 1,770(120) = $212,400, subcontracting cost is 130(180) = $23,400, and inventory cost is (120 + 250)(15) = $5,550, for a total minimum cost of $241,350. The earlier inventory substitutes for more expensive subcontracting in quarter 4. The schedule is independently solved with HiGHS."}],"tables":[{"caption":"One optimal schedule (units)","headers":["Quarter","Demand","Regular","Subcontract","Ending inventory"],"rows":[["1","580","450","130","0"],["2","300","420","0","120"],["3","320","450","0","250"],["4","700","450","0","0"]]}]},"compare":["Is demand met in its own quarter rather than merely over the whole year?","Do regular and subcontracted quantities stay within every quarter’s limits?","Are initial and final inventory zero and every inventory nonnegative?","Does the cost include regular production, subcontracting and end-of-quarter holding?","Recalculate the objective at the displayed schedule and compare all three cost components; do not omit inventory held before the peak-demand quarter."]},{"id":"practice-hw5-dea","title":"Compare the efficiency of six manufacturing companies","lectureIds":["modeling-examples"],"topicIds":["dea"],"kind":"homework","assignment":"Homework 5 · problem 1","sources":["Assignment5.pdf · page 1 (official problem 1; derived solution)"],"prompt":"Six manufacturing companies use three inputs to generate two outputs. The inputs are annual net value of fixed assets, working capital and number of employees. The outputs are total output value and total profit and tax. Using the data below, formulate and evaluate a Data Envelopment Analysis model and rank the companies by how efficiently they convert inputs into outputs. The assignment does not specify a returns-to-scale convention; state the convention used in your model.","tables":[{"caption":"Enterprise performance data","headers":["Company","Fixed assets","Working capital","Employees","Output value","Profit & tax"],"rows":[["A",4,15,8,60,12],["B",15,4,2,39,6],["C",27,5,5,24,13],["D",15,7,6,16,7],["E",8,13,12,51,14],["F",13,4,3,44,10]]}],"tasks":["Choose and state a DEA convention, define its variables and formulate one evaluation LP for each company.","Solve the six LPs, report the efficiency scores and rank the companies; explain ties rather than inventing a strict ordering.","If using solver code, retain the full formulation and inspect feasibility and solver status. The original problem requests the model and evaluation; it does not prescribe a particular solver for this part."],"hints":[{"title":"Avoid mixing incompatible units","body":"A single unweighted sum of all inputs or outputs is not an efficiency measure. DEA introduces weights or compares the company with a nonnegative reference combination of peers."},{"title":"Normalize or scale the evaluated company","body":"In a multiplier model, normalize the evaluated company’s weighted input and maximize its weighted output. In an envelopment model, hold outputs at their required levels while contracting inputs."},{"title":"State the convention before ranking","body":"An input-oriented constant-returns CCR model is one explicit reader choice. Keep peer comparisons consistent across all companies and do not add a convexity constraint unless you intend a different model."}],"solution":{"label":"Derived worked solution","body":"This is a reader-derived worked solution based on the instructor’s problem data, not an official answer key. Numerical results have been independently checked.","sections":[{"title":"Input-oriented envelopment formulation","body":"For evaluated company o, use nonnegative reference weights lambda_j and input factor theta. Let xij be input i at company j, and yrj output r.\n$$\\min\\theta;\\qquad\\sum_jx_{ij}\\lambda_j\\le\\theta x_{io}\\ (i=1,2,3),\\qquad\\sum_jy_{rj}\\lambda_j\\ge y_{ro}\\ (r=1,2),\\qquad\\lambda_j,\\theta\\ge0.$$\nThere is no sum_j lambda_j = 1 constraint in the constant-returns model. Self-reference gives a feasible efficiency of one."},{"title":"Equivalent weight formulation and ranking","body":"Choose nonnegative output weights u1,u2 and input weights v1,v2,v3. For company o maximize u1*y1o + u2*y2o, normalize v1*x1o + v2*x2o + v3*x3o = 1, and require every company's weighted output minus weighted input to be <= 0. Each company chooses its most favorable admissible weights. Both LP forms give the same scores.\n$$A=B=C=E=F>D,\\qquad\\theta_D=959/1824\\approx0.525767544.$$\nD's reference uses lambda_E = 217/1824 and lambda_F = 973/1824, with all other weights zero. Its radial input saving is 47.4232%. The five score-one companies remain tied; a stricter ordering would require an additional method or restrictions not specified in the assignment."},{"title":"Reading the score table","body":"Under the stated input-oriented CCR convention, companies A, B, C, E and F have efficiency one, while D has 959/1824, approximately 0.525767544. The ranking is A = B = C = E = F > D. This does not provide a strict ordering within the efficient group. D’s reference activity uses lambdaE = 217/1824 and lambdaF = 973/1824. Its radial input contraction is 1 - thetaD = 47.4232%, while maintaining at least its existing outputs."}],"tables":[{"caption":"Derived input-oriented CCR scores","headers":["Company","Efficiency"],"rows":[["A",1],["B",1],["C",1],["D","959/1824 = 0.525767544"],["E",1],["F",1]]}],"downloads":[{"title":"Complete COPT exercise code for Homework 5","href":"assets/examples/assignment5_copt.py"}]},"compare":["Is the returns-to-scale and input/output orientation explicit?","Does each company receive a separate LP with all six companies represented in the peer constraints?","Does the normalization use the currently evaluated company’s own inputs?","Are weights/reference quantities nonnegative?","Compare scores only under the same convention. A score of one can leave several companies tied; different DEA conventions can yield different evaluations."]},{"id":"practice-hw5-allen","title":"Resolve Allen’s investment example using COPT","lectureIds":["modeling-examples"],"topicIds":["portfolio"],"kind":"homework","assignment":"Homework 5 · problem 2","sources":["Assignment5.pdf · page 2 (official problem 2)","chapter04-2.pdf · pages 8-9 (investment data and model-summary context)"],"prompt":"The assignment asks you to solve the investment example from lecture 4, page 8, using COPT. Kathleen Allen has $70,000 for a one-year investment period. Municipal bonds may receive at most 20% of total investment. CDs cannot receive more than the other three alternatives combined. Treasury bills and CDs together must receive at least 30% of the total and at least 1.2 times the amount allocated to bonds and the stock fund. Annual return rates are below. Allocate the fund to maximize annual return.","tables":[{"caption":"Investment returns","headers":["Option","Annual return"],"rows":[["Municipal bonds","8.5%"],["Certificates of deposit","5%"],["Treasury bills","6.5%"],["Stock fund","13%"]]}],"tasks":["Define the dollar-allocation variables and write the investment LP using the lecture’s stated data and restrictions.","Implement and solve the model with COPT, checking the optimal solver status.","Report all four allocations and the maximum one-year return; include the complete script used to produce the result."],"hints":[{"title":"Match variables to the four investments","body":"Use the same investment order throughout the objective, constraints and result table. The fund gives one budget equality."},{"title":"Translate the grouped restrictions","body":"CDs plus treasury bills are compared once with total funds and once with bonds plus stock. The 1.2 condition is a ratio between groups, not a 20% share of total funds."},{"title":"Separate return from final wealth","body":"Apply the annual return percentages to the allocated dollars. Do not add the principal inside the return objective; check solver status before reading its variable values."}],"solution":{"label":"Derived worked solution","body":"This is a reader-derived worked solution based on the instructor’s problem data, not an official answer key. Numerical results have been independently checked.","sections":[{"title":"Formulation","body":"Let x1,x2,x3,x4 be dollars in those four alternatives.\n$$\\max Z=0.085x_1+0.05x_2+0.065x_3+0.13x_4.$$\n$$x_1+x_2+x_3+x_4=70000,\\quad x_1\\le14000,\\quad x_2-x_1-x_3-x_4\\le0,$$\n$$x_2+x_3\\ge21000,\\quad-1.2x_1+x_2+x_3-1.2x_4\\ge0,\\quad x_1,x_2,x_3,x_4\\ge0.$$\nThe full COPT exercise code is available with the assignment materials."},{"title":"Derived COPT result","body":"The allocation is (0,0,38181.818182,31818.181818), with annual return $6,618.181818, or 9.454545% of capital. Its year-end value including principal is $76,618.181818. Treasury bills dominate CDs, and stock dominates municipal bonds without relaxing any constraints. The ratio x3 >= 1.2x4 then binds. This independently checked result is a worked derivation, not an official answer key."}],"tables":[{"caption":"One-year allocation and annual return","headers":["Investment","Amount ($)","Return rate"],"rows":[["Municipal bonds",0,"8.5%"],["CDs",0,"5%"],["Treasury bills","38181.818182","6.5%"],["Stock fund","31818.181818","13%"]]}],"downloads":[{"title":"Complete COPT exercise code for Homework 5","href":"assets/examples/assignment5_copt.py"}]},"compare":["Do allocations total $70,000, with nonnegativity?","Are the bond cap, CD comparison, 30% group minimum and 1.2 ratio all included?","Are return coefficients matched to the correct investments?","Does your COPT run finish with optimal status, and do reported values come from that run?","Compare annual return with annual return; principal plus return is a different displayed quantity."]},{"id":"practice-hw5-biofuel","title":"Blend biofuel grades under two quality limits","lectureIds":["modeling-examples"],"topicIds":["blending"],"kind":"homework","assignment":"Homework 5 · problem 3","sources":["Assignment5.pdf · page 2 (official problem 3; derived COPT solution)"],"prompt":"A refinery can purchase up to the feedstock quantities below and allocate them between Premium and Standard bio-diesel. Quality attributes are volume-weighted averages. Premium sells for $72/barrel, requires cetane at least 58 and sulfur no greater than 0.11%, and must have production of at least 3,000 barrels. Standard sells for $58/barrel, requires cetane at least 50 and sulfur no greater than 0.18%, and must have at least 4,000 barrels. Maximize sales revenue minus feedstock purchase costs.","tables":[{"caption":"Feedstock purchase and quality data","headers":["Feedstock","Availability (barrels)","Cost ($/barrel)","Cetane rating","Sulfur content"],"rows":[["A",4000,42,65,"0.08%"],["B",5000,36,52,"0.15%"],["C",3500,30,45,"0.25%"]]},{"caption":"Grade requirements","headers":["Grade","Sale price ($/barrel)","Minimum production (barrels)","Minimum cetane","Maximum sulfur"],"rows":[["Premium",72,3000,58,"0.11%"],["Standard",58,4000,50,"0.18%"]]}],"tasks":["Part (a): define the blending variables and formulate a linear program for total net profit, including availability, minimum production and both quality restrictions for each grade.","Part (b): solve with COPT and provide the complete Python script.","Report every feedstock-to-grade allocation and the maximum net profit. Check total grade volumes and weighted-average quality values."],"hints":[{"title":"Index by feedstock and grade","body":"Use one variable for each of the six allocations. A grade’s volume is the sum of its three feedstock quantities."},{"title":"Linearize the quality averages","body":"Multiply each average-quality condition by its grade’s total volume. The specified minimum grade production makes that total positive. Keep sulfur units consistent in every term."},{"title":"Charge purchases once","body":"Use sale price minus feedstock cost as each allocation’s objective coefficient, or separate total revenue and total purchase cost. Availability is an upper bound on purchases, not a requirement to buy every available barrel."}],"solution":{"label":"Derived worked solution","body":"This is a reader-derived worked solution based on the instructor’s problem data, not an official answer key. Numerical results have been independently checked.","sections":[{"title":"(a) Linear formulation","body":"Let xiP,xiS >= 0 be barrels of feedstock i in Premium and Standard, and QP = xAP + xBP + xCP, QS = xAS + xBS + xCS.\n$$\\max Z=30x_{AP}+36x_{BP}+42x_{CP}+16x_{AS}+22x_{BS}+28x_{CS}.$$\n$$x_{AP}+x_{AS}\\le4000,\\quad x_{BP}+x_{BS}\\le5000,\\quad x_{CP}+x_{CS}\\le3500,\\quad Q_P\\ge3000,\\quad Q_S\\ge4000.$$\n$$65x_{AP}+52x_{BP}+45x_{CP}\\ge58Q_P,\\quad65x_{AS}+52x_{BS}+45x_{CS}\\ge50Q_S.$$\n$$0.08x_{AP}+0.15x_{BP}+0.25x_{CP}\\le0.11Q_P,\\quad0.08x_{AS}+0.15x_{BS}+0.25x_{CS}\\le0.18Q_S.$$\nSulfur coefficients are consistently in percentage points. All purchase quantities are optional up to their upper bounds, so charge only feedstock actually used."},{"title":"(b) One derived optimal COPT allocation","body":"The table gives one optimum; allocations need not be unique. Grade totals are 4,357.142857 Premium and 8,142.857143 Standard barrels. Their cetane ratings are 61.470588 and 50.309598; sulfur contents are exactly 0.11% and 0.18%. All feedstock is used. Revenue is $786,000, purchase cost $453,000, and maximum net profit $333,000. The downloadable script prints full-precision output and saves it as JSON. The result was independently checked with HiGHS."},{"title":"A short optimality bound","body":"Let a,b,c be total feedstock purchases. Adding the sulfur rows yields 0.07QP <= 0.10a + 0.03b - 0.07c. Therefore profit <= 36a + 28b + 14c <= 36(4000) + 28(5000) + 14(3500) = 333000. The feasible reported allocation attains the bound."}],"tables":[{"caption":"One optimal blending plan (barrels)","headers":["Feedstock","Premium","Standard","Total"],"rows":[["A","3588.235294","411.764706","4000"],["B","0","5000","5000"],["C","768.907563","2731.092437","3500"]]},{"caption":"Derived grade totals and quality checks","headers":["Grade","Volume (barrels)","Cetane","Sulfur"],"rows":[["Premium","4357.142857","61.470588","0.11%"],["Standard","8142.857143","50.309598","0.18%"]]}],"downloads":[{"title":"Complete COPT exercise code for Homework 5","href":"assets/examples/assignment5_copt.py"}]},"compare":["Are there six nonnegative allocation variables?","Does each feedstock’s use across both grades stay within its purchase limit?","Are both grade minimum quantities included?","Do cetane rows use >= and sulfur rows use <= for the correct grade averages?","Are sulfur percentages consistently scaled?","A different optimal blend can be correct: compare feasibility, maximum net profit and solver status, rather than requiring every allocation to match the example."]},{"id":"practice-hw5-transshipment","title":"Route plant supplies through constrained distribution hubs","lectureIds":["modeling-examples"],"topicIds":["transport"],"kind":"homework","assignment":"Homework 5 · problem 4","sources":["Assignment5.pdf · page 2 (official problem 4; derived COPT solution)"],"prompt":"Two plants supply three markets through two intermediate hubs. Every shipment must pass through exactly one of the hubs; direct plant-to-customer deliveries are not allowed. Plants P1 and P2 can supply at most 800 and 1,000 units. Hubs T1 and T2 can handle at most 900 and 850 units. Hubs store no inventory, so incoming units must equal outgoing units. Customer demands are 500, 650 and 450. Unit costs for all allowed routes are below. Find the minimum-cost shipment plan.","tables":[{"caption":"Plant-to-hub unit cost ($)","headers":["From","T1","T2","Supply upper bound"],"rows":[["P1","5","7","800"],["P2","6","4","1000"]]},{"caption":"Hub-to-customer unit cost ($)","headers":["From","C1","C2","C3","Throughput upper bound"],"rows":[["T1","8","10","12","900"],["T2","11","7","9","850"]]},{"caption":"Customer requirements (units)","headers":["Customer","C1","C2","C3"],"rows":[["Demand","500","650","450"]]}],"tasks":["Part (a): define plant-to-hub and hub-to-customer flow variables and formulate a linear program with plant limits, hub capacities, hub flow balance and exact market demands.","Part (b): solve the model using COPT and provide the complete Python script.","Report the flow on every allowed route, including zero-flow routes, and the minimum total transportation cost."],"hints":[{"title":"Use two layers of arcs","body":"Create variables only for plant-to-hub and hub-to-market routes. Do not introduce direct plant-customer arcs."},{"title":"Balance each hub separately","body":"For each hub, equate total plant inflow with total market outflow. Apply its throughput capacity to either total; do not double-count a unit by adding inflow and outflow."},{"title":"Distinguish available supply from demand","body":"Plant quantities are upper bounds, but each customer’s requirement must be met exactly. Compute cost across both legs of every shipment."}],"solution":{"label":"Derived worked solution","body":"This is a reader-derived worked solution based on the instructor’s problem data, not an official answer key. Numerical results have been independently checked.","sections":[{"title":"(a) Linear formulation","body":"Let aih >= 0 be units from plant i to hub h and bhk >= 0 units from hub h to customer k. Minimize total arc cost using the two tables. Plant constraints are ai1 + ai2 <= supply_i. Hub inflow equals outflow and is at most capacity_h. Customer inflow equals demand_k.\n$$\\min Z=5a_{11}+7a_{12}+6a_{21}+4a_{22}+8b_{11}+10b_{12}+12b_{13}+11b_{21}+7b_{22}+9b_{23}.$$\n$$a_{11}+a_{12}\\le800,\\quad a_{21}+a_{22}\\le1000,\\quad a_{11}+a_{21}\\le900,\\quad a_{12}+a_{22}\\le850.$$\n$$a_{11}+a_{21}=b_{11}+b_{12}+b_{13},\\quad a_{12}+a_{22}=b_{21}+b_{22}+b_{23}.$$\n$$b_{11}+b_{21}=500,\\quad b_{12}+b_{22}=650,\\quad b_{13}+b_{23}=450.$$\nHub capacity counts a unit once; inflow and outflow describe the same unit passing through the hub."},{"title":"(b) One derived COPT optimum","body":"Send 750 units P1->T1, zero P1->T2, zero P2->T1 and 850 P2->T2. T1 sends (500,250,0) to C1,C2,C3; T2 sends (0,400,450). Every flow is nonnegative and demand is exact. Cost is 5(750) + 4(850) + 8(500) + 10(250) + 7(400) + 9(450) = $20,500. T2 is full; T1 has 150 spare units. Plant spare capacities are 50 and 150. This is a derived result checked with COPT and HiGHS, not an official answer key."},{"title":"Why the minimum is attained","body":"Ignoring capacities, the cheapest full routes to C1,C2,C3 cost $13,$11,$13. Markets C2 and C3 need 1,100 units, but T2 carries at most 850. At least 250 of those units must use T1 at an extra cost of at least $4 each. Every feasible plan therefore costs at least 13(500) + 11(650) + 13(450) + 4(250) = $20,500. The reported plan attains that bound. The 250 T1 units for C2 and C3 can be divided differently while preserving this cost, so optimal flows are not unique."}],"tables":[{"caption":"One optimal plant-to-hub flow (units)","headers":["Plant","T1","T2"],"rows":[["P1",750,0],["P2",0,850]]},{"caption":"One optimal hub-to-market flow (units)","headers":["Hub","C1","C2","C3"],"rows":[["T1",500,250,0],["T2",0,400,450]]}],"downloads":[{"title":"Complete COPT exercise code for Homework 5","href":"assets/examples/assignment5_copt.py"}]},"compare":["Do you exclude direct plant-to-customer shipments?","Does every hub have its own inflow = outflow equality?","Is each hub’s throughput counted once and bounded by the stated capacity?","Are plant supplies upper bounds and customer requirements equalities?","Does the objective include costs on both route layers?","Alternative optimal flows are possible. Compare all feasibility conditions and the minimum total cost, with values obtained from an optimal COPT run."]}]};
